-- title:
--   Fibres of the fine family over algebraically closed fields
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are distinct primes, $N$ a nonzero level with $r \nmid N$, $\bar r \nmid N$ and $N$ squarefree.
--
--   **Base rings.** $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero discrete valuation domain with uniformiser $\pi$ ($\pi$ irreducible), $\pi$-adically complete, with residue ring of cardinality $r$ and with $(r) = (\pi)$; $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal O$. $Onr$ is a characteristic-zero $\mathcal O$-domain equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, with that ideal maximal, every element of $Onr$ satisfying a monic $\mathcal O$-polynomial modulo $\pi$, every monic polynomial of positive degree over $Onr$ having a root modulo $\pi$, and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod \pi$; thus $Onr$ plays the role of the completed maximal unramified extension with its Frobenius. Further, $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a homomorphism characterised by $v\!\det g = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ for some $u \in \mathcal O^{\times}$.
--
--   **Indefinite side and moduli.** $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ satisfies `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`: $a > 0$ or $b > 0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ the completion $\mathbb H \otimes \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ divides $r$ or $\bar r$. $\Lambda$ is a maximal order (an order that is maximal among orders containing it). The datum $(\mathcal X, f, \mathrm{pt})$ is a coarse moduli space over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O$ for the project's false elliptic curves `FakeEllipticCurve` $\Lambda$ $N$ (abelian-scheme data of relative dimension $2$ with a commutative relative group law, a $\Lambda$-action satisfying the trace condition, and level-$N$ subscheme data), in the sense of `IsCoarseModuli`: isomorphism invariance, compatibility with pullback, bijectivity on geometric points, and the usual universal property. For $n \geq 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N) = 1$, the datum $(M, f_M, \mathrm{ptF})$ is a fine moduli space for such curves with full level-$n$ structure (`IsFineModuli`: isomorphism invariance, pullback compatibility, and bijectivity of $\mathrm{ptF}$ on $S$-points for all $S$), and $(G,\rho,\chi)$ is a level-twisting action on it (`IsLevelTwistAction`: $\rho$ acts over the base, twisting a full level structure by $\chi(g)$ is implemented by $\rho(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, injective and surjective modulo $n$). The morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ lies over the base, is invariant under $\rho$, and carries $\mathrm{ptF}$ to $\mathrm{pt}$.
--
--   **Hecke tower and Atkin–Lehner data.** For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$, $(\mathcal Y_\ell, g_\ell, \mathrm{ptT}_\ell)$ is a coarse moduli space for curves with extra level-$\ell$ structure (`IsCoarseModuliT`), with degeneracy maps $d_0, d_1 : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over the base, $d_0$ forgetting the $\ell$-structure and $d_1$ recording the $\ell$-isogeny quotient. The endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base implement the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points.
--
--   **Definite side.** $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ satisfies `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt rbar` ($a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, ramified exactly at $\bar r$); $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order, $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$) contained in $\Lambda_1$; $n_1$ is an idelic unit in `primeHeckeSet` $R_1$ $r$ such that $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1 = R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$; $\iota_0$ is an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$; $v$ is the height-one prime containing $r$. The subgroup $\tilde\Gamma = \Gamma t$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^{\times}$ is `awayUnits` $R_1$ $v$, the units integral at every place other than $v$. For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$ elements $s_\ell \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ and ideles $sf_\ell$ are given with the four conditions `hs`: the component of $sf_\ell$ at each place not above $r$ is $s_\ell \otimes 1$; the component at the place above $r$ is $1$; the product of the diagonal idele of the scalar $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet` $\Lambda_1$ $(\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1)$ $\ell$ when $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet` $(\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R_1\,n_1)$ $\ell$ otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$. The groups $\Gamma t_\ell = \tilde\Gamma \cap s_\ell \tilde\Gamma s_\ell^{-1}$ are fixed by `hΓtℓ`, and $\bar w$ is a unit of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\tilde\Gamma$.
--
--   **Coordinates and special fibre.** $\Lambda$ contains all integers ($h\Lambda\mathbb Z$) and $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to W(\mathbb F_{r^2})^2$ is an order coordinate system (`IsOrderCoord`: additive, unital, with the prescribed twisted multiplication rule, injective, with dense image and the trace condition). Over the residue ring $Onr/\pi$ there are given a false elliptic curve $A_0$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ (`FormalODModule`, `HasHeight 4`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ for $A_0$, families of endomorphisms $e, e' : \tilde\Gamma \to \mathrm{End}(A_0)$ over the base with degrees $\deg$, and the hypothesis that $A_0$ is a formal module via $(\mathrm{coord}, X_0, \theta_0)$. The hypotheses `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`, `hE2`, `hE3`, `hE4` (summarised here) assert: each pair $(e\gamma, e'\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg \gamma}$ with $e\gamma$ level-preserving; $e$ is multiplicative and respects scalars up to powers of $r$, and conversely an $e\gamma$ which is multiplication by a positive integer up to $r$-powers forces $\gamma$ to be a rational scalar; for every injective embedding of the centraliser of the $X_0$-actions into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ for which each $e\gamma$ is induced on $\theta_0$ by a formal endomorphism $\varepsilon$ with image $r^{k_\gamma}\,g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; each full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$ is moved by $e\gamma$ by a label $\mathrm{lab}(\gamma) \in \Lambda$, multiplicative and scalar-compatible modulo $n$; and every $r$-power isogeny endomorphism of a curve pulled back from $A_0$ over an algebraically closed field comes, up to $r$-powers, from some $e\gamma$. The block `hE5a`–`hE5e` (summarised here) provides an order $R_2 \leq R_1$ with $r$-power denominators over $R_1$ together with a realisation $\hat e : R_2 \to \mathrm{End}(A_0)$ compatible with the group law, with the $\Lambda$-action and with levels, unital, multiplicative, agreeing with $\Lambda$-multiplication on integers, satisfying $\hat e(\bar x)\hat e(x) = \mathrm{nrd}(x)$, compatible with $e$ on $r$-power multiples of elements of $\tilde\Gamma$, and admitting a joint matrix realisation for $\tilde\Gamma$ and $R_2$ simultaneously.
--
--   **Drinfeld side.** $\iota : W(\mathbb F_{r^2}) \to Onr$ is a ring map, $\Phi$ a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module of height $4$ over $Onr/(r)$, $MD$ a moduli package over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf, and $\eta$ assigns to each rigidified object a point of $MD$; the three clauses of `hη` state that on admissible rigidifications $\eta$ is injective up to isomorphism, that $\eta$ is natural in the base ring, and that every point of $MD$ is locally in the image of $\eta$ on a Zariski cover. $E_0$ is an injective ring map from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-actions to $M_2(K_0)$ whose image is commensurable with $M_2(\mathcal O)$ in the precise sense of `hE₀` (there is $m$ with $r^{m}M_2(\mathcal O)$ inside the image and $r^{m}$ times the image inside $M_2(\mathcal O)$). The transformation $eD$, with `hnatD`, `hbijD`, `hfstD`, `hGLD`, `hPiD`, `hPiexD`, is a natural bijection from the functor $\mathrm{ModuliPackage.G}\,\mathcal O\,MD$ to the product of $\mathrm{Spec}$-corepresentable functor of $Onr$ with the Deligne-datum functor $\Omega(K_0,\pi)$, preserving the first (leg) component, translating the action of $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ into `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` and the $\pi$-translate into the Frobenius twist of the leg, the latter always existing.
--
--   Further, $\kappa$ is a ring map $Onr/\pi \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps, and $\beta_0$ an isogeny of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to $X_0$ transported along $\kappa$; $P_0$ is a full level-$n$ structure on $A_0$. The data $A_{0w}, a_w, a_w', b_w, b_w'$ exhibit $A_{0w}$ as the Atkin–Lehner quotient of $A_0$ at $\bar r$ together with an $r^{k_w}$-isogeny pair back, level-preserving, and `hJOINTw` gives a joint matrix realisation of $\tilde\Gamma$ and of the composite $a_w$ followed by $b_w$ as $\iota_0(\bar w)$ up to powers of $r$.
--
--   **The rigidified comparison.** $\Xi$ attaches to each rigidified curve over a Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, relative to a leg $\psi : Onr \to B$, a point of $\mathrm{GPoint}\,\mathcal O\,MD$. The hypotheses `hΞleg`, `hΞnat`, `hΞiso`, `hΞdef` state: over a connected base the leg of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist of $\psi$; $\Xi$ is compatible with pullback of curves and rigidifications; $\Xi$ is unchanged by an isomorphism of rigidified curves in the stated comparison form; and, given a formal-module structure on the curve, $\Xi$ is computed by $\eta$ on an admissible rigidified object obtained by rigidity transport with $j \leq 1$ and leg $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$.
--
--   Finally $g_0 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ is fixed, and the following are assumed (contents summarised): `heqΓ` and `heqW`, that translating a rigidification by $e\gamma$, respectively passing to the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$ in the displayed comparison form, corresponds under $\Xi$ to the action of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$, respectively $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\bar w)g_0^{-1}$, in the sense of `ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy`; `hK1`–`hK4`, that pairs (leg, Deligne datum) normalised by $g_0^{-1}$ are realised by rigidified curves, that two such realisations are isomorphic in the displayed rigidified sense, and that the realisation is compatible with base change along $\varphi$ and lifts along a surjection with square-zero kernel; `hC0e` and `hC0u`, existence and uniqueness of the normalised level-$n$ transport $P_n$ of $P_0$ along a rigidification; the family $\Theta_f$ sending a triple (leg, Deligne datum, element of $G$) to a $\pi$-nilpotent point of $f_M$, natural in the base (`hnat`) and satisfying $\rho(h)$-equivariance in the form that $\rho(h)$ carries $\Theta_f(x, gh)$ to $\Theta_f(x,g)$ (`hG`); `hC2` and `hC2c`, that for matching data $\Theta_f((\psi,P),1)$ is the point $\mathrm{ptF}$ of the pair (curve, normalised level structure) and that composing it with $p$ gives $\mathrm{pt}$ of the curve; a monoid homomorphism $\theta_t : \tilde\Gamma \to G$; `hC3`, the translation clause, that a twisted action by $\iota_0(\gamma)$ on (leg, datum) is realised by a second rigidification of the same curve, whose normalised level structure differs from the first by the action of $\chi(\theta_t(\gamma))$ and whose extra level-$\ell$ structures, for primes $\ell \neq r$ invertible in the base, correspond exactly when the reference structure on $A_0$ is stable under $e\gamma$; and `hC5`, the one-leg curve-level fibre clause: over an algebraically closed field, if two curves rigidified over the same leg realise $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ and are isomorphic by $i$ (in the sense of `IsoVia`), then there is $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ whose twisted action by $\iota_0(\gamma)$ carries $(\psi,P)$ to $(\psi,P')$, which matches the normalised level-$n$ structures after twisting by $\chi(\theta_t(\gamma))$ and transporting along $i$, and for which, at each prime $\ell \neq r$ invertible in the field, the transported extra level-$\ell$ structures correspond under $i$ if and only if the reference structure on $A_0$ is stable under $e\gamma$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every algebraically closed field $k$ that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, for all $\mathcal O$-algebra maps $\psi, \psi' : Onr \to k$, all Deligne data $P, P'$ over $k$ and all $g, g' \in G$, the equality
--   $$\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi',P'),g')$$
--   holds if and only if there exists $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ such that the following three conditions hold.
--
--   First, $g' = \theta_t(\gamma)\,g$.
--
--   Second, $P'$ is the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$ in the sense of `DeligneDatum.IsPullback`: for every full $\mathcal O$-lattice $M$ in $K_0^2$, the line of $P'$ at $M$ is the preimage, under the base-changed action isomorphism, of the line of $P$ at $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}M$.
--
--   Third, the two legs agree after a Frobenius twist on the relevant invariants: for every $y \in Onr$ such that $\mathrm{Fr}^{\,v\det \iota_0(z)}(y) = y$ for all $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ which are rational scalars and satisfy $\theta_t(z) = 1$, one has $\psi'(y) = \psi\big(\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det \iota_0(\gamma)}(y)\big)$.
--
--   This is the fibre-identification step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation of the Shimura curve attached to the indefinite quaternion algebra ramified at $r$ and $\bar r$: it says that two points of the formal family $\Theta_f$ over an algebraically closed residue-characteristic-$r$ field coincide exactly when the second triple (leg, Deligne datum, level element) is a $\tilde\Gamma$-translate of the first, the curve-level input being taken in its one-leg form. It feeds the assembly step [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_levelHom_translate_fibre_of_fineFamily_of_isNoetherianRing_heightNormalised_conn_eq_oneLegC5`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_levelHom_translate_fibre_of_fineFamily_of_isNoetherianRing_heightNormalised_conn_eq_oneLegC5).
